<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eblana</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eblana is a place named on the east coast of Ireland in the Geography of Ptolemy, compiled around 150 CE, as a town of the Eblani people. Older writers took it to be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, but most scholars now doubt the identification because Dublin's name is Irish and Ptolemy's coordinates for Ireland are imprecise. Its true location is unknown.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geography-ptolemy">Geographia</a> of the Greek astronomer and cartographer Claudius Ptolemaeus, Eblana does not stand on a river but between the mouths of two, the Buvinda, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-boyne">River Boyne</a>, and the Oboka. Early antiquaries who took Eblana for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> identified the Oboka with the river entering the sea at Arklow in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-wicklow">County Wicklow</a>, which they therefore called the Ovoca, now the River Avoca. The Oboka seems rather to be the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-liffey">River Liffey</a>, with Ptolemy's Modonnos probably the Avoca, which places Eblana somewhere between the mouths of the Boyne and the Liffey, in the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-dublin">County Dublin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Early Irish antiquarians such as Sir John Ware and Walter Harris held that Eblana was a corruption of Deblana, a form of the Gaelic Dubh Linn (Black Pool), from which the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> derives, and Ptolemy did drop the first letters of other names, writing Epidii for Pepidii and Ulcinium for Dulcinium. The 19th-century writer Louis Agassiz used Eblana as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name for Dublin. Against this, the earliest Gaelic settlement on Dublin's site was called Áth Cliath (Ford of Hurdles), and Duiblinn first appears as the name of a Christian church settlement that cannot predate the 5th century.</p><p>Eoin MacNeill argued that Eblana could not be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>, since Irish records and tradition know of nothing like a city there before the Norsemen fortified the site in 841, and placed it farther north, probably on the coast of Louth. More recent scholarship favours the seaside village of Loughshinny, close to the promontory fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drumanagh">Drumanagh</a>, probably a trading post with links to Roman Britain and sometimes described as a possible bridgehead for a Roman invasion; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> burials on nearby Lambay Island have yielded Romano-British brooches and metalwork. Another candidate is the mouth of the Delvin River, with its chamber tombs, but its Irish name Ailbhine never appears with -bl-. No proof ties Eblana to any place.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eblana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He places them on the east coast of Ireland, south of the Voluntii and north of the Cauci, roughly in the area of modern County Dublin and Meath, with a town called <b>Eblana</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/eblani">Eblani</a></li></ul></section>
